  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe 20Z/2 pm surface map shows 1025-1027 mb high pressure over\nthe mid and upper MS river valley and providing ample sunshine\nto central/se IL today. Blanket of stratocumulus clouds with\nMVFR bases were ne of Gary, Lafayette and Indy metro. Few to \nscattered cumulus clouds were over southeast Crawford and \ncentral and northeast Lawrence counties. North to NW winds were \naround 10 mph or less, much lighter than yesterday. Cool temps \nwere in the mid to upper 40s. Aloft an omega block pattern was \nover the country with strong 529 decameter 500 mb low in \nsouthern Quebec Province (north of Montreal) and another deep \nupper level low/trof off the Pacific Northwest coast while sharp\nskinny ridge over the central Plains from east Texas northward\ninto Manitoba Province.  \n\nClear skies, light to calm winds and dry air mass to bring\nanother widespread hard freeze to CWA overnight with lows in the\nmid 20s common. Some shallow/patchy freezing fog possible near\nrivers/large water areas late tonight into mid Sunday morning\nespecially IL river, but feel coverage too limited to mention at\nthis time. \n\nWith the blocky weather pattern in place over the U.S., the \nsurface high pressure ridge to slowly shift eastward from the MS\nriver valley into the Ohio River Valley on Monday and into the \nmid Atlantic States on Wed. Dry conditions to likely continue \nacross CWA through at least Wed night and possibly into Friday. \nSome high clouds to spread into central IL especially IL and MS \nriver valley on Sunday with more cirrus closer to the MS river. \nStill a mostly sunny day in store over area on Sunday with light\nto calm winds and highs in the lower 50s. Another hard freeze\nexpected Sunday night with lows in the mid to upper 20s. Sunny\nskies expected Monday and Tuesday across area with temps slowly\nmodifying. Highs Monday in the mid 50s with light winds, and \nupper 50s on Tuesday with south winds 5-10 mph central IL and \nlight in southeast IL. \n\nUpper level ridge shifts into the Southeast States Wed-Friday\nwith a northern stream short wave trof ejecting into the upper\nMS river valley and northern Great Lakes Wed night/Thu. Brunt of\nits moisture and lift to pass north/nw of central IL and just\nhave slight pops far western/nw CWA on Thu. Meanwhile a stronger \nupper level trof digs into the Great Plains and upper Midwest by\nFriday night and Saturday. This to push a cold front eastward\ninto IL late Sat or into Sunday morning. GFS model appears too\nquick with front moving in Sat afternoon while Ecmwf is slower\nwith front moving into area Sunday morning. NBM shows pops of\nshowers increasing from nw to se during Saturday to 30-50% by\nSat afternoon over much of central IL with slight pops in\nsoutheast IL Saturday afternoon, though dry in far se CWA on\nSaturday. Temps continue to warm with increasing southerly flow\nduring 2nd half of week, with highs around 60F to lower 60s Wed,\nlow to mid 60s Thu, 60s Friday and upper 60s to around 70F Sat.\n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Nov\n22-28th has a 45-55% chance of above normal temperatures across\nIL with precipitation trending near normal. So pleasant late\nfall weather looks to continue the next two weeks over\ncentral/se IL.",
